1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:09,485
Pulse filters. Which records reach the rest of the request, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:26,634
By default, filters run second, right after features. They read source fields and feature columns. Attributes come later, so their labels are out of reach, with one exception: the previous-state attribute runs before the filters, and an expression filter can read it.

3
00:00:27,434 --> 00:00:35,890
Each filter names a type, a field and its values. Values are always strings: labels for categories, numbers for numeric fields.

4
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:45,829
An unknown label is refused before a single row is read, and the request fails. A filter adds no column. It only keeps or drops.

5
00:00:46,629 --> 00:00:52,461
Filters run in the order you list them. Each one sees only the records the previous filter kept.

6
00:00:53,261 --> 00:01:01,696
Every filter reports three counts: what it saw, what it let through, and how many arrived with the field null, whether it kept them or dropped them.

7
00:01:02,496 --> 00:01:09,459
Reorder them and the survivors stay the same, but the counts move, because each filter now sees a different stream.

8
00:01:10,259 --> 00:01:16,006
Include is an allow-list. A record stays only if its value is one of the values you name.

9
00:01:16,806 --> 00:01:20,099
A null region is not on the list, so include drops it.

10
00:01:20,899 --> 00:01:27,904
Exclude is a block-list, and here's the surprise: a null isn't on the block-list either, so exclude lets it through.

11
00:01:28,704 --> 00:01:34,707
It still counts as a null input. That count covers every null the filter saw, kept or dropped.

12
00:01:35,507 --> 00:01:43,686
A range filter keeps values between a low and a high bound. Both ends are inclusive, so eighteen and sixty-five both stay.

13
00:01:44,486 --> 00:01:49,721
A null age has no position on the line, so it's dropped and counted as a null input.

14
00:01:50,521 --> 00:01:57,164
Dates are matched as day numbers since nineteen seventy. For an open-ended range, use an expression instead.

15
00:01:57,964 --> 00:02:06,569
Date ranges keep records whose date falls inside any labelled span. Spans are inclusive, and a missing end means open-ended.

16
00:02:07,369 --> 00:02:19,494
Write the spans inline, or name a range table registered with the instance. Overlapping spans or duplicate labels are refused. A null date is dropped, and counted as a null input.

17
00:02:20,294 --> 00:02:29,070
A datetime is first floored to its local day. Eleven-thirty at night in UTC is already the first of March in Berlin, so it's kept.

18
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:40,609
With no zone set, the day is UTC's, so the same instant is still February and dropped. A per-row zone field works too, and a row with a null zone is dropped and counted.

19
00:02:41,409 --> 00:02:49,929
True keeps records whose flag is set. By default it's strict, and only works on boolean fields. Truthy mode works on any scalar type.

20
00:02:50,729 --> 00:02:59,505
False in truthy mode keeps false, zero, empty and null. Strict mode would drop that null instead. Kept or dropped, the null is counted.

21
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:06,585
The null filter reads only the null bitmap. Not-a-number, or an empty set, is a value, not a null.

22
00:03:07,385 --> 00:03:15,542
Multi-select fields are stored as bitmasks: one bit per option. The set filters compare each row's mask with a query mask.

23
00:03:16,342 --> 00:03:24,713
Contains any keeps rows that share at least one option. Contains all needs every option. Contains none needs no overlap at all.

24
00:03:25,513 --> 00:03:35,462
Equals needs exactly the same selection. A null set is dropped by all of them except contains none, and an empty set is a real answer, not a null.

25
00:03:36,262 --> 00:03:43,417
When no single filter fits, write an expression. Categories read as their labels, and sets read as lists of labels.

26
00:03:44,217 --> 00:03:53,078
The expression is compiled once, so a typo fails before any row is read, with a formula compile error, at predict and at the run alike.

27
00:03:53,878 --> 00:04:01,459
Careful with nulls. A null compared with greater-than gives an unknown answer, and the row is dropped. Guard with a default.

28
00:04:02,259 --> 00:04:08,944
Pass values as params, and read them as params dot name. Change the value, never the expression text.

29
00:04:09,744 --> 00:04:18,243
Calendar helpers, like weekday and month index, read dates as days. A datetime needs to-day first, or the compile refuses it.

30
00:04:19,043 --> 00:04:28,288
And one attribute is readable: the previous-state attribute runs before the filters, so an expression can keep the subjects who went from trial to paid.

31
00:04:29,088 --> 00:04:37,800
Here is the whole picture. Most filters drop a null, but exclude, contains none, false in truthy mode and the is-null check all keep it.

32
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:45,819
Kept or dropped, every null a filter sees lands in its null input count, so you can always see where the nulls went.

33
00:04:46,619 --> 00:04:53,539
Filters are built to be cheap. Each decides from one row, so they stream, and their counts add up across shards.

34
00:04:54,339 --> 00:05:05,142
Set filters are a single bitwise test. Expressions compile once. On grouped cohorts, a filter can be answered once per dictionary entry, instead of once per row.

35
00:05:05,942 --> 00:05:11,859
That's the filter toolkit today: membership, ranges, dates, flags, nulls, sets and expressions.

36
00:05:12,659 --> 00:05:20,368
More filters are added over time, and embedders can register their own. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

37
00:05:21,168 --> 00:05:25,443
Remember: know what each filter does with a null. Next up, attributes.

38
00:05:26,243 --> 00:05:27,510
Pulse filters.
